  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Big temperature swing ahead with near-record \nwarmth today and frost and freeze expected this weekend\n\nMax temperatures are still projected to challenge daily records\ntoday and Wednesday - esp today. 8H temps get around 18C this\nafternoon, Most everyone in Central PA will get to 75F with a \nfew going over 80F. However, temps Wed will probably hold about\n10F cooler than today in the NW/Allegheny Plateau. The SE could\nget a couple degs warmer Wed than today. But, isolated/scattered\nshowers Wed could hold temps down a couple of degs from current\nguidance. \n\nFriday will be the first shot of truly colder air we will\nexperience this fall. A cold front will pass through with temps\ndropping down near freezing Sat AM in the nrn mtns even though\nit may be cloudy all (Fri) night there - meaning it could \nfreeze without a frost occurring. Then, Sunday AM looks like \neveryone could frost up - with many places in the N and Laurels\ngoing sub-30F. \n\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Windy and wet end to the week with first flakes\n\nWhile there will be a few SHRA Wed, mainly S of I-80, the more\nlikely period of widespread wet weather comes Thursday night \nand Friday. An amplified upper trough will cross the Great \nLakes and spin up a deep sfc low in the Gulf of Maine and \nCanadian Maritimes. While broader in overall coverage, late week\nrainfall amounts will be on the light side ranging from around \n0.50 inch in the NW Alleghenies to 0.10 inch in the Lower \nSusquehanna Valley. \n\nGusty winds up to 40 mph are forecast on the backside of the \ndeep low and will deliver much colder air downwind of the Great\nLakes this weekend. This pattern will be favorable for lake \neffect rain and possibly snow showers later Friday into early \nSaturday over the higher elevations. NBM data even suggests a \nminor snow accum (<0.5 inch) is possible in the NW mtns and \nhighest ridgetops/summits in the Laurel Highlands. Land/road\ntemps will likely be warm enough to keep any accums to the \ngrass and mulch, not the roads.",
